TLDR: Implemented #177, refactored/removed unnecessary imports/added typing for files I came across or directly relating, added tests including testing the required output file flag (assuming functionality of throwing error when -o flag not set which was implied)

I implemented the TOML marshalling and unmarshalling using the library:

```
"@iarna/toml": "^2.2.3",
```

Because it was the only one that had the stringifying and parsing functionalities needed and seemed well tested.

I had an issue with the deepmerge library that was used in the project for my specific case. The problem I had was if an empty object (which had a parameter defined and defaulted to be undefined) was deep merged with an object that had that type defined, it would not be merged and instead kept the empty object value.

for example:

```Typescript
db: {
    name: options.db
},
```

would create (when options.db was undefined):

```
"db":{}
```

and when merged with:
```
"db":{"name":"..."}
```
would end up being:
```
"db":{}
```

And when trying to keep the heirarchy of `cli options > config file options > defaults` there would be inconsistencies and unintended functionality. Workaround for this was to handle the db.name and eth1.contract.address specifically (because they both came from cli and/or defaults):
```Typescript
interface IConfigFile extends JsonMap{
  db?: {name: string};
  chain?: {chain: string};
  rpc?: {port: string};
  eth1?: {contract?: {address: string}};
}
...
    let parsedConfig: IConfigFile;
    if (options.configFile) {
      let data: Buffer;
      try {
        data = fs.readFileSync(options.configFile);
      } catch {
        throw new CliError(`${options.configFile} could not be parsed.`);
      }
      parsedConfig = parse(data.toString());
    }
      
    let optionsMap: BeaconNodeCtx = {
      db: {
        name: options.db || parsedConfig ? parsedConfig.db.name : defaults.db.name
      },
...
```

and deepmerge the rest after. I avoided handling each value specifically and still relying on deepmerge for the others (since all others worked as intended which leads me to believe it is because name was defined for that object but unset).  

Currently, the config file only saves the chain, db, rpc port, and eth1 contract address but it can be extended to include any others from the eth1 and p2p object defaults easily (I ignored other variables because the implementations were not final and I personally didn't see the need to have those config saved).

Other notes:
- I changed --depositContract abbreviation to -dc to allow config file flag to be -c
- Created interfaces for config options to allow the objects to be passed into the action function without destructuring or defaulting to any type
- Fixed a few bugs (can elaborate if you want specifics)
- Added types where missing
- Fixed other linter warnings in directly relating files
- Created basic test for if the command line function was added and that it throws the error when output flag is not set

Implements #207 

generates key pair with:

```Typescript
const keyPair = bls.generateKeyPair();
```

Converts to hex string with:

```Typescript
export function blsPrivateKeyToHex(privateKey: PrivateKey): string {
  const byteBuffer = Buffer.alloc(48, 0);
  privateKey.getValue().tobytearray(byteBuffer, 0);
  const pkBytes = byteBuffer.slice(16, 48);

  return "0x".concat(pkBytes.toString('hex'));
}
```

and then saves object with private key to file:

```Typescript
    const object = {
      privateKey: blsPrivateKeyToHex(keyPair.privateKey),
      // publicKey: keyPair.publicKey.toHexString()
    }

    try {
      fs.writeFileSync(options.outputFile, JSON.stringify(object, null, 2));
    } catch (err) {
      throw new CliError(`Failed to write to ${options.outputFile}: ${err}`);
    }
```

Testing includes basic registration of CLI command, testing to make sure writing cannot happen to existing file, and testing temporary bls key to hex conversion function as follows:

```Typescript
describe("BLSPrivateKeyToHex conversion", () => {
  it(" generated private key should equal original", () => {
    const keyPair = bls.generateKeyPair();
    const hexString = blsPrivateKeyToHex(keyPair.privateKey);
    const remadeKey = PrivateKey.fromHexString(hexString);
    assert(hexString === blsPrivateKeyToHex(remadeKey));
  });
});
```

That test is inefficient because it converts the privateKey to hex one extra time than necessary for comparison, but this was because I didn't know a good way to compare the two `BIG` variables together reliably.

Ignores publicKey for now, but I commented out the line that can be added in very easily to give a hex of the public key stored in the JSON file as well.

