#### Problem
Reviewing https://github.com/ChainSafe/lodestar/pull/1241 I found un-scalable that the CLI structure had to be hardcoded in the CLI docs generation script. The ideal scenario would be for the script to consume the CLI itself without having to redefine it's structure again, which would make maintainability problematic.

Yargs does not offer any way to access it's internal cmd structure or registry in a way that would allow automatic docs to be generated. Nor there seem to be any tool out there that do so.

#### Proposed solution

We have a good standard way of defining commands. If we define then in a consumable format instead of using the yargs builder, the resulting command tree object can be both consumed by the documentation buidler and yargs itself. I propose to define commands with:

```ts
export interface ICliCommand<OwnOptions = {}, ParentOptions = {}> {
  command: string;
  describe: string;
  options?: Required<{[key in keyof OwnOptions]: Options}>;
  // 1st arg: any = free own sub command options
  // 2nd arg: subcommand parent options is = to this command options + parent options
  // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
  subcommands?: ICliCommand<any, OwnOptions & ParentOptions>[];
  handler: (args: OwnOptions & ParentOptions) => void;
}
```

The key is that subcommands are recursively available, instead of being fed into the yargs blackbox with `yargs.command` in the command parent builder. It also achieves better type safety, as it will enforce that a command's `OwnOptions` match its options object.

:warning: This PR is a demo with this structure applied to only two commands. **Code owners** reply with yes / no / comments, and if the idea is liked I will apply it to the rest of the commands.

#### To docs

After implementing this, the script of https://github.com/ChainSafe/lodestar/pull/1241 can fed the command data structure and generate the docs without having to manually import and declare everything.

